  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThe core idea is to leverage a structured initialization with a periodic binary pattern and enhance it with a more rigorous smooth approximation of the max operator. This approach addresses the non-convexity by approximating the non-differentiable maximum with a differentiable surrogate, which allows for more effective gradient-based optimization. The bottleneck of previous methods\u2014getting stuck in local minima\u2014is mitigated by introducing structured perturbations and a hybrid optimization strategy combining gradient descent with explicit constraint enforcement. The binary pattern initialization ensures a balanced distribution of mass and reduces autocorrelation at key shifts.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nThe function h must satisfy:\n- 0 \u2264 h(x) \u2264 1 for all x\n- \u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x) dx = 1\n\nThe objective is to minimize C\u2085 = max_k \u222b h(x)(1 - h(x +k)) dx, where the integral is approximated via np.correlate and scaled by dx. To handle the non-differentiable max operator, we approximate it using the smooth surrogate:\n\nC\u2085_approx = (1/\u03b1) * ln(\u03a3 exp(\u03b1 * overlap_k)) \n\nwhere \u03b1 is a large constant (e.g., 1000) to approximate the max. This allows gradient-based optimization. The surrogates are evaluated alongside the exact evaluator for final checks.\n\nThe constraint is \u2211h[i] = n_points / 2. The optimization uses SLSQP with bounds [0, 1] and equality constraint \u2211h[i] = target_sum. The exact objective function is re-evaluated at each step to ensure fidelity to the original metric.\n\n3. Implementation procedure\n- Parent Validation: If initial_h_values is provided, it is used to start the search. Otherwise, initialize with a sparse, periodic binary pattern: set h[i] = 1 at positions i \u2261 floor(n_points / 4) mod (n_points / 2), ensuring the integral is met.\n- Optimization Loop:\n   - Use SLSQP with the log-sum-exp surrogate to minimize C\u2085_approx.\n   - After each iteration, evaluate the exact objective function and store the best solution found.\n   - Introduce structured perturbations by shifting the binary pattern or swapping 0/1 positions to escape local optima.\n   - Refine the solution using L-BFGS-B after initial optimization to improve convergence.\n- Candidate Acceptance: Track the best solution found, ensuring 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 and \u2211h[i] = target_sum.\n- Termination: Stop when the time budget is exhausted or when no improvement is observed for 10 iterations.\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\n- Default configuration: Use a binary pattern with 1s spaced evenly across the interval, initialized with \u03b1=1000 for the surrogate. Use SLSQP for 200 iterations with 100 subiterations, then L-BFGS-B for refinement.\n- Perturbation: Shift the binary pattern by \u00b11/4 of the interval or randomly swap 0/1 positions every 10 iterations.\n- Variations: Test with different binary pattern spacings (e.g., 1/3, 1/2 of the interval), \u03b1 values (100-1000), and solver combinations (SLSQP + L-BFGS-B).\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\n- The optimization loop runs for 200 iterations, with each SLSQP call taking approximately 0.5s. This consumes 100s, leaving time for refinement.\n- If the objective becomes unstable (nan values), clip h to [0,1] and rescale. For solver failures, revert to the best solution thus far.\n- The fallback parent is used if the initial_h_values is available, ensuring valid feasibility.\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n- Re-evaluate the final candidate using the exact objective and verify \u2211h = target_sum.\n- Ensure all h[i] \u2208 [0,1] and the C\u2085 bound is finite.\n- Output (h_values, c5_bound, n_points) with n_points=200, ensuring all constraints are satisfied.",
